          %h3#KineticAction The kinetic action and Planck’s constant
          %p
            To naturally see why there would be any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            associated to a (to some)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
            in the first place, we step back and consider
            %em local trivializations
            or
            %em local potentials
            for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+forms"} symplectic forms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+quantization"} geometric quantization
            is called
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            and what in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematics"} mathematics
            is called lifting to
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            Given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-symplectic+form"} pre-symplectic form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %mo ∈
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega \in \Omega^2_{\mathrm{cl}}(X)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+lemma"} Poincaré lemma
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+open+cover"} good open cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{U_i \hookrightarrow X\}_i
            such that one can find smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+1-forms"} differential 1-forms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i \in \Omega^1(U_i)
            such that these are local trivializations/potentials for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            on each patch
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            of the cover:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{d}\theta_i = \omega_{|U_i}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Physically such a 1-form is (up to a factor of 2) a choice of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+energy"} kinetic energy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density"} density
            called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+Lagrangian"} kinetic Lagrangian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\mathrm{kin}}
            (below in example
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StandardPrequantizationOfStandardR2PhaseSpace"}
            we connect this statement to a maybe more familiar formla):
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                      %mo ,
                      %mi i
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta_i = 2 L_{\mathrm{kin}, i}
                  \\,.
          #StandardPrequantizationOfStandardR2PhaseSpace.num_example
            %h6#example_7 Example
            %p
              Consider the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo ,
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi ω
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R}^2, \; \omega = \mathbf{d} q \wedge \mathbf{d} p)
              of example
              = succeed "." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CanonicalR2PhaseSpace"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible topological space
              we consider the trivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covering"} covering
              (
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^2
              covering itself) since this is already a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/good+covering"} good covering
              in this case. Then all the
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{g_{i j}\}
              are trivial and the data of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              consists simply of a choise of 1-form
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi Ω
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \in \Omega^1(\mathbb{R}^2)
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi q
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi p
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbf{d}\theta = \mathbf{d}q \wedge \mathbf{d}p
                    \\,.
            %p A standard such choice is
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∧
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\theta = - p \wedge \mathbf{d}q
                    \\,.
            %p
              Then given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi γ
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma \colon [0,1] \longrightarrow X
              which satisfies
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamilton%27s+equation"} Hamilton's equation
              for a standard
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+energy"} kinetic energy
              term, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mi γ
                      %mo ˙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p \mathbf{d}q)(\dot\gamma)
              is this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+energy"} kinetic energy
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle"} particle
              which traces out this
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trajectory"} trajectory
          %p
            Given a path
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma : [0,1] \to X
            in phase space, its
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/kinetic+action"} kinetic action
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{\mathrm{kin}}
            is supposed to be the integral of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{\mathrm{kin}}
            along this trajectory. In order to make sense of this in the generality where there is no globally defined
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∩
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi j
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{i j} \in C^\infty(U_i \cap U_j, \mathbb{R})
            for each double intersection of patches of the cover, such that these the local
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi θ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivative"} derivative
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+cohomology"} de Rham
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{d}
            ) of these functions:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi j
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\theta_j|_{U_j} - \theta_i|_{U_i} = \mathbf{d}g_{i j}
                  \\,.
          %p
            One then finds (from the theory of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cech+cohomology"} Cech cohomology
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi ij
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  g_{ij} + g_{j k} = g_{i k}
          %p then there is a well defined action functional
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_{\mathrm{kin}}(\gamma) \in \mathbb{R}
          %p
            obtained by dividing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma
            into small pieces that each map to a single patch
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i
            along this piece, and adding the contribution of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{i j}
            at the point where one switches from using
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i
            to using
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_j
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holonomy"} holonomy
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parallel+transport"} parallel transport
            of the
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R},+)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            which is defined by the data
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\theta_i\}, \{g_{i j}\} )
          %p
            However, requiring this condition on triple overlaps as an equation between
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi θ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta \in \Omega^1(X)
            and functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_i \in C^\infty(U_i, \mathbb{R})
            such that
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mi θ
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                    %mo +
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \theta_i = \theta|_{U_i} + \mathbf{d}h_i
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mn 0
                        %mo ,
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %msup
                      %mi γ
                      %mo *
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{\mathrm{kin}} = \int_{[0,1]} \gamma^\ast L_{\mathrm{kin}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is exact, which is not the case in many important examples. (In more abstract terms what this is saying is that every
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{R},+)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            over a manifolds is trivializable.)
          %p
            On the other hand, what really matters in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantum+field+theory"} prequantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is not the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} kin
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{\mathrm{kin}} \in \mathbb{R}
            itself, but the
            %em exponentiated
            action
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                    %mi S
                    %mo )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp\left( \tfrac{i}{\hbar} S \right) \in \mathbb{R}/(2\pi \hbar)\cdot\mathbb{Z}
                  \\,,
          %p
            which takes values in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of the additive group of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integral
            multiples of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\pi \hbar
          %p In more detail, consider the canonical inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{Z} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            as an addiditve
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subgroup"} subgroup
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+numbers"} real numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            on which a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            is chosen as part of the identification of mathematical formalism with physical reality, one should really consider
            %em all
            possible additive group homomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}\to \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  h \in (\mathbb{R}- \{0\}) \hookrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi h
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (-)\cdot h \;\colon\; \mathbb{Z} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
          %p
            and this “physical
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h
            is what is called
            = succeed "." do
              %em Planck’s constant
          %p
            In particular the induced
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
            is identified as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h \mathbb{Z}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi h
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  U(1) \simeq \mathbb{R}/h \mathbb{Z}
          %p
            and under this identification its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            map is expressed in terms of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+function"} exponential function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi z
                  %mo ↦
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %mn ∞
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi z
                        %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo !
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp \colon z \mapsto  \sum_{k = 0}^\infty \frac{z^k}{k!} \in \mathbb{C}
            as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\exp(2 \pi \tfrac{i}{h}(-))
                  \=
                  \\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} (-)) \;\colon\; \mathbb{R} \longrightarrow U(1)
                  \\,,
          %p where
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hbar \coloneqq h/2\pi
                  \\,.
          %p
            The resulting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
            is the real
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential+exact+sequence"} exponential exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi exp
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                        %mfrac
                          %mi i
                          %mi ℏ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  0 \to \mathbb{Z} \longrightarrow \mathbb{R} \stackrel{\exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar}(-))}{\longrightarrow} U(1) \to 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is the source of the ubiquity of the expression
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mi i
                      %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} (-))
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            appears as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi exp
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi i
                        %mi ℏ
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\tfrac{i}{\hbar} S)
          %h3#Prequantization Pre-Quantization and Differential cohomology
          %p
            By the above discussion, for the exponentiated kinetic action functional to be well defined, one only needs that the equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi k
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g_{i j} + g_{j k} = g_{i k}
            on triple intersection holds modulo addition of an integral multiple of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi h
                    %mo =
                    %mn 2
                    %mi π
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h = 2\pi \hbar
          %p
            If this is the case, then one says that the data
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\theta_i\}, \{g_{i j}\})
            defines equivalently
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                a
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            %li
              %p
                a degree-2 cocycle in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+differential+cohomology"} ordinary differential cohomology
          %p
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            the given symplectic 2-form
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
          %p
            Such data is called a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pre-quantization"} pre-quantization
            of the symplectic manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mfrac
                    %mi i
                    %mi ℏ
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exp(\frac{i}{\hbar} S)
            that enters the quantization of the given mechanical system (for instance as the integrand of a
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integral"} path integral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
            of a symplectic manifold is indeed precisely the data necessary before quantization.
          %p Therefore, in the spirit of the above discussion of pre-symplectic structures, we would like to refine the smooth moduli space of closed differential 2-forms to a moduli space of prequantized differential 2-forms.
          %p
            Again this does naturally exist if only we allow for a good notion of “space”. An additional phenomenon to be taken care of now is that while pre-symplectic forms are either equal or not, their pre-quantizations can be different and yet be
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalent
          %p
            because there is still a remaining freedom to change this data without changing the exponentiated action along a
            %em closed
            path: we say that a choice of functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi i
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mn ∞
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi U
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mi π
                  %mi ℏ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h_i \in C^\infty(U_i, \mathbb{R}/(2\pi\hbar)\mathbb{Z})
            defines an equivalence between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\theta_i\}, \{g_{i j}\})
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi g
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\tilde \theta_i\}, \{\tilde g_{i j}\})
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi θ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                    %mi i
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi d
                  %msub
                    %mi h
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde \theta_i - \theta_i = \mathbf{d}h_i
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi g
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi g
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi j
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tilde g_{i j} - g_{i j} = h_j - h_i
          %p
            This means that the space of prequantizations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ω
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
            is similar to an
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold"} orbifold
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            of pre-quantum structures on a manifold
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            In just the same way then that above we found a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+set"} smooth
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+space"} moduli space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi Ω
                    %mi cl
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Omega}^2_{cl}
            of closed differential 2-forms, one can find a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
            (for more on this see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics"} geometry of physics
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "geometry%20of%20physics#SmoothnGroupoids"} Smooth homotopy types
            ), which we denote
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} conn
                  %mo ∈
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{\mathrm{conn}}
                  \\in
                  \\mathbf{H}
          #PrequantumGaugeTransformation.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_8 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoid"} smooth groupoid
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}U(1)_{\mathrm{conn}}
              is
              %em characterized
              as follows,
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  For
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                  a
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∇
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi conn
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\nabla \colon X \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                %p
                  are equivalent to the above prequantum data
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi θ
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mrow
                            %mi i
                            %mi j
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\theta_i\}, \{g_{i j}\})
                  on
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi X
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              %li
                %p
                  for
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mn 2
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi X
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mi conn
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_1, \nabla_2 \colon X \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                  two such maps,
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopies"} homotopies
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mrow
                          %mtable
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↗
                                %msup
                                  %mo ↘
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mo ∇
                                        %mn 1
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                                %mi X
                              %mtd
                                %mo ⇓
                              %mtd
                                %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                                  %mi B
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mn 1
                                %msub
                                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                                  %mi conn
                            %mtr
                              %mtd
                              %mtd
                                %mo ↘
                                %msub
                                  %mo ↗
                                  %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                                    %mrow
                                      %msub
                                        %mo ∇
                                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        \\array{
                        \&amp; \nearrow \searrow^{\mathrlap{\nabla_1}}
                        \\\
                        X &amp; \Downarrow &amp; \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                        \\\
                        \&amp; \searrow \nearrow_{\mathrlap{\nabla_2}}
                        }
                %p
                  between these are equivalent to the above
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformations"} gauge transformations
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi i
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{h_i\})
                  between this data
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 2
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                    %mo −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                      %mfrac
                        %mi ℏ
                        %mi i
                    %mi log
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi h
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\theta_2)_i - (\theta_1)_i = \mathbf{d} \tfrac{\hbar}{i} log (h_i)
                    \\,.
          #UniverselCurvatureMap.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_9 Proposition
            %p
              There is a universal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              map, a morphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+groupoids"} smooth groupoids
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} conn
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Ω
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cl
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F
                    \\;\colon\;
                    \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{\mathrm{conn}}
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathbf{\Omega}^2_{\mathrm{cl}}
            %p
              which is such that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi conn
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \colon X \longrightarrow \mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
              a
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mo ∇
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi conn
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_\nabla \;\colon\;
                    X
                    \\stackrel{\nabla}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\mathbf{B}U(1)_{conn}
                    \\stackrel{F_{(-)}}{\longrightarrow}
                    \\mathbf{\Omega}^2_{cl}
            %p
              is its
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              2-form.
          %p
            Hence this is the map that sends
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi θ
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\{\theta_i\}, \{g_{i j}\})
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            with
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ω
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mi i
                    %mo =
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi d
                    %msub
                      %mi θ
                      %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega|_{U_i} = \mathbf{d}\theta_i
          %p Therefore:
          #Prequantization.num_defn
            %h6#definition_11 Definition
            %p
              A
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+manifold"} symplectic manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ω
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              is – if it exists – a choice of
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+group"} circle group
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+connection"} principal connection
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              whose
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
              2-form is the given
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectic+form"} symplectic form
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo =
                    %mi ω
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    F_\nabla = \omega
                    \\,.
          %p
            In terms of the classifying morphism of differential forms as in prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PresymplecticFormsAsMapsIntoASmoothSpace"}
            this reads as follows.
          #PrequantizationByLiftingClassifyingMap.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_10 Proposition
            %p
              Given a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+manifold"} presymplectic manifold
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi ω
                    %msubsup
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi Ω
                      %mi cl
                      %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %msubsup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Ω
                          %mi cl
                          %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \stackrel{\omega}{\longrightarrow} \mathbf{\Omega}^2_{cl}) \in \mathbf{H}_{/\mathbf{\Omega}^2_{cl}}
              by prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SymplecticManifoldsAsObjectsInSliceOverModuliOf2Forms"}
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/prequantization"} prequantization
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ω
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,\omega)
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#Prequantization"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              in
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo ⟶
                              %mo ∇
                          %mtd
                            %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                              %mi B
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mi conn
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
